.Im MicroEmacs modes
.so me.tni
.TH cr 2m
.XI cr - "File's carriage return (CR) style"
.SH NAME
cr \- File's carriage return (CR) style
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B "crlf Mode"
.sp
\fBc\fR \- mode line letter.
.SH DESCRIPTION
When  enabled  \fBcr\fR  indicates  that a carriage  return  should be written
out at the end of the line. Typically enabled independently for different line
ending styles used in \s-1DOS\s0  and  \s-1UNIX\s0.  When 1 then  \fB\\r\fR is
included in the line ending; when 0 then \fB\\r\fR is not included in the line
ending.
.SH NOTES
\fBcr\fR mode is used in conjunction with
.Ht lf 2m 
to control the line feed, \fBlf\fR alone giving Unix and both Windows line endings.
.PP
When a file is read every carriage return (\fB\\r\fR), line feed (\fB\\n\fR) or carriage return and line feed ends a
line, each line keeping its own line ending, and the most common line ending sets the \fBcr\fR and \fBlf\fR modes.
A line whose ending differs from the modes is written with its own ending so the file is saved unchanged, see
.Ht $file-checks 5
for the check made when such a file is saved. Changing either mode gives every line the new line ending. A typed or
inserted line takes the buffer's line ending, see
.Ht insert-file 2 .
.SH "SEE ALSO"
.na
